859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-x+5y)+(-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+7y)+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+7y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-7y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(0)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+1=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-7y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-1+0-7\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-7y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0+1-7\\cdot (-5)) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈-5.00,\\,y(1)≈12.00.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0dd15e47a99a1bb9ded57fdf081c20fef121aa27bf96de70b9ca6973e1f38b92"} {"question" "q-339859" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339859" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3.00"] ["math" "-4.81"] ["math" "-5.00"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339859" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5.00"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339859" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339859" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12.03"] ["math" "11.32"] ["math" "12.00"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339859" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12.00"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+5y,\\,y(1)=-2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x+5y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+9\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 13) \\\\  & =-124.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{3}{2})≈13.00,\\,y(2)≈-124.00.\n$$" "