" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-111.11"] ["math" "-122.32"] ["math" "-124.00"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339868" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-124.00"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x+4y,\\,y(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(0)$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(0)≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((3x+4y)+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(0)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+1=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 0+6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2} \\\\  & =-10.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈3.00,\\,y(1)≈-10.50.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0dd15e47a99a1bb9ded57fdf081c20fef121aa27bf96de70b9ca6973e1f38b92"} {"question" "q-339771" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339771" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3.21"] ["math" "2.89"] ["math" "3.00"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339771" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.00"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339771" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339771" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10.50"] ["math" "-9.50"] ["math" "10.03"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339771" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-10.50"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3x+y,\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})$ and $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈{{field-1}},\\,y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-3x+y)+(-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{4})$ and $y(\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+9\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4} \\\\  & =3.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4},\\,y=3.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+9\\cdot (3.75)) \\\\  & =4.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{4})≈3.75,\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈4.50.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0dd15e47a99a1bb9ded57fdf081c20fef121aa27bf96de70b9ca6973e1f38b92"} {"question" "q-339869" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339869" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2.89"] ["math" "3.21"] ["math" "3.75"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339869" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.75"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339869" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339869" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4.50"] ["math" "4.99"] ["math" "5.01"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "q-339869" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.50"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/state.json" "file_sha256" "b96a57c5f8f6e3aaee3877a2d45be8f978e12e7e10cf9dcac74f571889b1efd0"} {"question" "e-21672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x^2 - 2y, \\qquad y(3) = -1\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $\\Delta x = 4.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_\\text{new},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value $x_\\text{new}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/example-21672.json" "file_sha256" "6874e9ab859cf12ba9c4a4d83003ddf5808744c0fc6bc3e9f64f73be41d1985d"} {"question" "e-21672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{x}^{2}-2y+(3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+6{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-4y-4{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+6{x}^{2}+6{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-4y-4{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =6{x}^{2}+6({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3y-4{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{\\text{new}} & =6{x}^{2}+6({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(6{x}^{2}+6({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3y)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/example-21672.json" "file_sha256" "6874e9ab859cf12ba9c4a4d83003ddf5808744c0fc6bc3e9f64f73be41d1985d"} {"question" "e-21671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2x - y, \\qquad y(1) = 0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right),$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/example-21671.json" "file_sha256" "3937964d18181038d72169fed661a985407eed5b09296df2c952faef54978257"} {"question" "e-21671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac43,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac43 - 1 = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-y+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = 1 + \\dfrac13 = \\dfrac43.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+5\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & ≈0.67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right) \\approx 0.67.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/example-21671.json" "file_sha256" "3937964d18181038d72169fed661a985407eed5b09296df2c952faef54978257"} {"question" "e-21673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x - y, \\qquad y(0) = 0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/example-21673.json" "file_sha256" "316f7424b65e708a319dd315bc0ae97a2e53dd38bdcf17196db8051d343ad4dd"} {"question" "e-21673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-0}2 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((4x-y)+(4{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(1)$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0, \\qquad y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + 1 = 1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & ≈1.33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=1, \\qquad y=1.33.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2+1.33) \\\\  & ≈4.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 1.33,\n\\qquad\ny(2) \\approx 4.44.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/example-21673.json" "file_sha256" "316f7424b65e708a319dd315bc0ae97a2e53dd38bdcf17196db8051d343ad4dd"} {"question" "q-333386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+2y}{2+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "fe88604b31ca71694116ebee7d1ed0a58e6f565ffae1271bc703eb26a1e0c981" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "With step size 1, the trapezoidal rule gives y_new=y+(e^x−3xy+e^(x_new)−3x_new·y_new)/2. Multiplying by 2 and collecting y_new terms yields (2+3x_new)y_new=e^x+e^(x_new)−3xy+2y. Division gives original option c (index 2) wherever the denominator is nonzero. The authentic worked solution agrees. Submitted option b was graded Incorrect; this is a derived source-backed judgment, not successful grading of option c. The stored new subscripts denote the same labels as the captured typeset subscripts." "bindings" {"problem" "c937296ee5741f9b0c6d77876c0e969a75b0ebc0760be3256ba94b0c25f01331" "worked_solution" "e76e82479d6e4acff952f86caf9d1da2c171f739fddd3c1349c9b8e41beb85a2" "original_field" "f7c050825ec12c17fafcd308d9f67be40dae55faabce1f673ec1e6d78986481a" "reviewed_field" "da90a51e50a481287e25e5a30d02048a08dd3a7e6628975c3a46bb95e5d5263a" "raw_widget" "faed606af2f8663b254aaa7246cff356381c4ec8def82c319e125c8c130df762"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-333386-before.json" "sha256" "9df1e7068a88dfecfccaf1bad234764000b0d01368098080216c5a124745cd0e"} {"path" "q-333386-before.png" "sha256" "696cc44ee58f3c323c4630814a864f2a824877bfa4213188d1d25a9c95a5130d"} {"path" "q-333386-after.png" "sha256" "c2cc64b7bef1b9f89d1adaa6a59efae67a20aae2ff2f877903ac3cf3b957078f"} {"path" "history-q-33